% 2026-10-17 (user): prose rewritten in the style of Zhang, Zhang, Cucuringu, and Qian (2024).
\begin{abstract}
% 2026-10-17 (user, new-notes 1): parked -- the target is built from front-month E-mini futures (03_data, Realized moments). Was: "forecasts of the realized variance of the S\&P 500"
% 2026-10-25 (user: lineage in the realized volatility literature; the alternative-data angle, not overdone): parked
% -- the opening sentence. Was: "We investigate whether exogenous predictors improve intraday forecasts of a
% futures-based (E-mini) proxy for the realized variance of the S\&P 500 over the closing half-hour interval
% (15:30--16:00 ET)."
Carrying the realized volatility literature to an intraday interval, we investigate whether exogenous predictors,
drawn from the intraday trading of the index's constituents and from alternative data such as social-media
sentiment and option order flow, improve intraday forecasts of a futures-based (E-mini) proxy for the realized
variance of the S\&P 500 over the closing half-hour interval (15:30--16:00 ET).
% 2026-10-22 (user): parked -- the pooled models are estimated on the 13 regular-hours bars (E34). Was: "Specifically, we estimate each model across all half-hour intervals and evaluate the forecast issued at 15:30 for the closing half hour over the out-of-sample period from"
Specifically, we estimate pooled models on the \rhePoolNBars{} regular-hours half-hour bars of each trading day
with a rolling window of \rhorWindowDays{} trading days and evaluate the forecast issued at 15:30 for the closing
half hour over the out-of-sample period from
% 2026-10-20 (user): parked -- Table 1 scored from 2012 (TABLE1_SWITCH.md, approved). Was: "\rhoosSpanStart{} to \rhoosSpanEnd{} ($T_{\mathrm{oos}} = \rhoosDays$ trading days). We compare a benchmark"
\rhoosSpanStart{} to \rhoosSpanEnd{} ($T_{\mathrm{oos}} = \rhoosDays$ trading days), and the forecasts of every
regular-hours bar from \rhorSpanStart{} to \rhorSpanEnd{} (\rhorDays{} trading days). We compare a benchmark
heterogeneous autoregressive (HAR) predictor set against an augmented predictor set that incorporates exogenous
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- "lower ... across all" no longer holds with the pooled linear models on the trees' design; the count is printed. Was: "series. Under the QLIKE loss, the full predictor set yields lower average out-of-sample loss than the HAR benchmark across all \rhrAfPoolPairs{} intraday model classes, with \rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} reductions statistically significant at the 5\% level."
% 2026-10-23 (user, final rerun, phase 2): parked -- on the final rerun the closing-half-hour QLIKE is lower with the full set for the three tree ensembles only and higher for the three penalized linear models (06_results, sec:rh_results_predictors); the every-bar result of Table 1, the sample the abstract names second, is added. Was: "series. Under the QLIKE loss, the full predictor set yields a lower average out-of-sample loss than the HAR benchmark in \rhrAfPoolLower{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} intraday model classes, with \rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} of these reductions statistically significant at the 5\% level. Furthermore, although every intraday specification remains less accurate under QLIKE than a"
% 2026-10-23 (phase 2): "all ... each significant" backed by \rhorAfLower = \rhorAfLowerSig = \rhorAfPairs (6), "none"
% by \rhrAfPoolLowerSig = 0 (generated/rehaul_oos_rerun_numbers.tex, rehaul_results_numbers.tex); counts in Section 6.
series. Under the QLIKE loss, scored on every regular-hours bar, the full predictor set yields a lower average
out-of-sample loss than the HAR benchmark in all \rhorAfPairs{} intraday model classes, each reduction statistically
significant at the 5\% level. For the forecast of the closing half hour, it lowers the loss in \rhrAfPoolLower{} of
the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} classes, the \rhrPoolNTreeWord{} tree ensembles, none of these reductions statistically
significant at the 5\% level, and raises it for the \rhrPoolNLinWord{} penalized linear models.
% 2026-10-19 (user): parked -- the MSE clause, with the QLIKE/squared-error divergence bullet of 06_results.tex. Was:
% "\rhrAfPoolPairs{} reductions statistically significant at the 5\% level, whereas mean squared error does not
% confirm these gains. Furthermore, ..."
% 2026-10-23 (final rerun): "every intraday specification remains less accurate" still holds: \rhrNLowerPool = 0 of
% the \rhrPoolN pooled forecasts have a lower QLIKE than the closing-interval HAR benchmark (make_rehaul_results.py).
Furthermore, although every intraday
specification remains less accurate under QLIKE than a
% 2026-10-18 (user, new-notes 11): parked -- a HAR-type model with its horizons, not a daily HAR regression; still the closing-interval OLS the comparison uses (make_rehaul_results.py:1276). Was: "daily HAR regression estimated solely on the closing interval, incorporating the exogenous predictors narrows"
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- "narrows this gap across all model classes"; the count is printed. Was: "... incorporating the exogenous predictors narrows this gap across all model classes."
HAR-type model in the spirit of \citet{Corsi2009}, estimated by OLS on the closing-interval observations of the target's trailing averages over $k \in \{1, 5, 25, 125, 625, 3125\}$ samples plus calendar columns, incorporating the exogenous predictors narrows
% 2026-10-25 (user: the option trades as an application of the variance forecasts): parked -- "In an economic
% application to zero-days-to-expiration (0DTE) S\&P 500 straddles on expiration days, a sign-based trading strategy
% achieves ..."
this gap in \rhrVsBmPoolNarrow{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} model classes, the tree ensembles. Because the target is
the variance of the closing half hour, the forecasts have a direct application: in zero-days-to-expiration (0DTE) S\&P 500
% 2026-10-23 (user, N1 / new-notes 15c, final rerun): parked -- "across every intraday model class" was typed for the 2012 design (note N15c); on the final rerun the count is printed (\rhrAfPoolShHigher, make_rehaul_results.py). Restore "every" only if it equals \rhrAfPoolPairs; the forest storyline (writeup/RF_STORY_OPTIONS.md) is not decided here. Was: "straddles on expiration days, a sign-based trading strategy achieves a higher Sharpe ratio under the full predictor set than under the HAR benchmark across every intraday model class."
% 2026-10-23 (user, N1 / N15c resolved, phase 2): parked -- the count; on the final rerun \rhrAfPoolShHigher = \rhrAfPoolPairs (all six classes; the MinGRU is parked), so "every" is restored, scoped to the classes and with the significance count (note N15c). Was: "... than under the HAR benchmark in \rhrAfPoolShHigher{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} intraday model classes."
straddles held over that half hour on expiration days, a sign-based trading strategy achieves a higher Sharpe ratio under the full
predictor set than under the HAR benchmark in every one of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} intraday model classes, in point
estimates; the gain is statistically significant at the 5\% level in \rhrAfPoolShHigherSig{} of them.
\end{abstract}

\editc{black}{Notes}{Colored notes are draft comments, not paper text.\\
\textbf{\textcolor{red!75!black}{Red}}: two live statements disagree.\\
\textbf{\textcolor{orange!75!black}{Orange}}: a sentence does not match the computation behind a printed number.\\
\textbf{\textcolor{blue!75!black}{Blue}}: a reader cannot recover the input or the rule.\\
The first line is the decision. The second line is the problem. The rest is the exposition.}

% 2026-10-19 (user): base 5 across the paper -- the fitted numbers that still come from base-2 designs.
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34): parked -- the note below, before the switch to the base-5 trees (writeup/pooled_trees_13.py); the new note follows it.
\iffalse
\editc{orange}{E34}{Decision: base 5 across the paper (user, 2026-10-19).\\Problem: the pooled trees (LightGBM, XGBoost and the random forest, on every predictor set) and the intraday OLS on the HAR benchmark (note a of Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos}) were fitted on the base-2 panel, horizons $\{1, 2, 4, \dots, 2048\}$. Every number built from them still comes from that design: the counts of intraday model classes in this abstract and in the introduction (\rhrAfPoolPairs{} classes, \rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} significant), the pooled tree and intraday OLS rows of Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos}, and the pooled comparisons of Sections~\ref{sec:rh_results} and~\ref{sec:rh_econ} and of the appendix.\\
The pooled ridge, lasso and elastic net printed here were already fitted at base 5 (experiments/pool\_linear\_pretune.py uses the production horizon set). % 2026-10-19 (user): parked -- "don't do the trees". Was: "The trees are refitted at base 5 on the new pooled design ... (note E15)."
The pooled trees are not refitted (user, 2026-10-19: not in this pass); the base-5 design for a refit exists (experiments/pooled\_trees\_base5.py, design stage; results/pooled\_trees\_base5\_2026-10-19\_48 and \_13 hold its column lists). The intraday OLS is replaced by the pooled OLS of the linear reruns. The generators of these numbers (make\_rehaul\_results.py, make\_rehaul\_oos\_table.py, make\_rehaul\_economics.py) still read results/pooled\_trees\_2026-10-07; switch them after the refits.}
\fi
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34): the pooled trees switched to the base-5 refit in every generator that prints them; what stays open is listed.
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- the Problem of the mixed designs; (ii) and (iii) are resolved. Was: "\\Problem: three inputs still come from earlier designs. (i) The model set (the \cmMasterN{} forecasts of the master table) still holds the earlier draft's pooled 48-interval forecasts (the ``paper'' family, among them its LightGBM and XGBoost), so the statistics computed over the model set in Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ} and in the appendix (the counts against the short straddle, the sizing and compounding medians, the interval-shift test) include them. (ii) The pooled linear models of Sections~\ref{sec:rh_results} and~\ref{sec:rh_econ} are the runs on all \rhdmNIntervals{} intervals with $W = \rhdmPooledLinWindow$ intervals, while the pooled trees are fitted on the \rheTreeNBars{} regular-hours bars with a window of \rheTreeWindowDays{} trading days, so the \rhrPoolN{} pooled intraday forecasts mix two designs. (iii) Table~\ref{tab:rh_dm_samples} and the \texttt{\textbackslash rhdm...} macros (writeup/make\_rehaul\_data\_methods.py, not run in this pass) still give the trees' window as $W = \rhdmPooledWindow$ intervals.\\"
% 2026-10-23 (user, E34, final rerun): parked -- the note on the 2012 design (the 1,000-day runs from 3 January 2012,
% results/pooled_trees_rerun13_2026-10-20/w1000 and results/pooled_linear_rerun_2026-10-20_13_2012_w1000); the final
% rerun (writeup/final_run.py, decided 23 October 2026) replaces it, and the new note follows the \fi.
\iffalse
\editc{orange}{E34}{Decision: base 5 across the paper (user, 2026-10-19); the pooled trees are the base-5 refit (user, 2026-10-22); the six pooled linear models of Sections~\ref{sec:rh_results} and~\ref{sec:rh_econ} are the forecasts of Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos} on the same design (user, 2026-10-22), so the \rhrPoolN{} pooled intraday forecasts share one design: the \rhePoolNBars{} regular-hours bars, a rolling window of \rhePoolWindowDays{} trading days, evaluation from \rhePoolRunStart.\\Problem: (i) the model set (the \cmMasterN{} forecasts of the master table) still holds earlier designs: the earlier draft's pooled 48-interval forecasts (the ``paper'' family, among them its LightGBM and XGBoost) and the master table's pooled linear runs on all \rhdmNIntervals{} intervals with $W = \rhdmPooledLinWindow$ intervals (marked ``All \rhdmNIntervals{} intervals'' in generated/rehaul\_results\_t3\_full.tex, not in the paper), so the statistics computed over the model set in Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ} and in the appendix (the counts against the short straddle, the sizing and compounding medians, the interval-shift test) include them. (ii) On the one design, the full predictor set lowers the closing-interval QLIKE in \rhrAfPoolLower{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} pooled classes (\rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} significantly) and raises the sign strategy's Sharpe ratio in \rhrAfPoolShHigher{} of them (note N15c); the sentences that said otherwise now print counts (writeup/E34\_OVERNIGHT\_REPORT.md). Table~\ref{tab:rh_dm_samples} gives the shared window.\\
The pooled trees (LightGBM, XGBoost and the random forest, on the HAR benchmark and on the full predictor set) are the refit on the design of the pooled linear models of Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos}: results/pooled\_trees\_rerun13\_2026-10-20/w1000 (CARC; branch insurance-selling-2026-09-19-trees-results, commit 6746103), base-5 HAR horizons, FOMC series in, the frozen configurations, re-estimated at every bar on a rolling window of \rheTreeWindowBars{} bars, from \rheTreeRunStart{} to \rhorSpanEnd. writeup/pooled\_trees\_13.py reads their 16:00 rows for make\_rehaul\_results.py, make\_rehaul\_economics.py and make\_rehaul\_oos\_table.py, and writeup/make\_rehaul\_oos\_rerun.py scores every bar of them in Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos} (note E36), so the counts in this abstract and in the introduction (\rhrAfPoolPairs{} classes, \rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} significant), the pooled comparisons of Sections~\ref{sec:rh_results} and~\ref{sec:rh_econ} and of the appendix now use them; the tree rows of Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos_last30} are closing-interval models and are unchanged. The intraday OLS of the earlier Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos} left the table with note E36. With the refit, the QLIKE reduction from the full predictor set is statistically significant at the 5\% level in \rhrAfPoolTreeLowerSig{} of the three tree classes (all three before), and the sentences that said each tree difference was significant now print counts (writeup/E34\_OVERNIGHT\_REPORT.md).}
\fi
\editc{orange}{E34}{Decision: one design for the \rhrPoolN{} pooled intraday forecasts (user, 2026-10-22), now the final rerun (decided 23 October 2026; writeup/final\_run.py, generators switched with REHAUL\_FINAL\_RUN=1): the \rhePoolNBars{} regular-hours bars, the base-5 HAR horizons, the \rhorNColsAf{} columns of the full predictor set, a rolling window of \rhePoolWindowDays{} trading days ($W = \rhePoolWindowBars$ bars) re-estimated at every bar, evaluation from \rhePoolRunStart; the linear penalties chosen once on \rhorPenSample{} and then frozen, and each tree method on its frozen configuration from the earlier Optuna search, not re-tuned. Inputs: the linear models from results/window\_sweep\_2014start\_2026-10-22/w3000; LightGBM and XGBoost from results/pooled\_trees\_final\_2014start\_w3000; the random forest, fitted on its frozen configuration with Intel's oneDAL engine (Intel Extension for Scikit-learn), from results/pooled\_rf\_skx\_2014start\_w3000 (RULE\_rf\_skx.txt). The abstract, the introduction and the conclusion state counts over these forecasts (note N15c, resolved 23 October 2026).\\Problem: the model set (the \cmMasterN{} forecasts of the master table) still holds earlier designs, the earlier draft's pooled 48-interval forecasts (the ``paper'' family, among them its LightGBM and XGBoost) and the master table's pooled linear runs on all \rhdmNIntervals{} intervals with $W = \rhdmPooledLinWindow$ intervals, so the statistics computed over the model set in the appendix include them. Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ} no longer uses it: since 23 October 2026 the interval-shift test and the compounded wealth run on the \rhpsNfc{} pooled forecasts (writeup/make\_rehaul\_pooled\_extras.py; notes E26, E27, N15b resolved).\\Robustness: the same runs at a window of \rhrbWinLong{} trading days are in Table~\ref{tab:rh_robust_w3500}. Pending: the pooled MinGRU (parked).}
% 2026-10-23 (user, E34, phase 2): parked -- in the note above, "... so the statistics computed over the model set in Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ} and in the appendix (the counts against the short straddle, the sizing and compounding medians, the interval-shift test) include them (notes E26, E27, N15b).\\Pending (2026-10-23): the robustness runs at the longer window (the random forest's walk at that window is not finished); the pooled MinGRU stays parked. The wording of the sentences whose direction depends on the final values is checked in the second pass."

